    {
      "dom_id": "question-277200",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$",
